========================================
Root Cause Analysis
========================================
The swizzle function:
  swizzle_a_row(logical_row, k) = logical_row ^ (((k >> 2) & 3) << 3)

caused two regressions:

1. Register pressure increase: 40 -> 42 registers/thread
   - The XOR/swizzle arithmetic added ~2 registers
   - Crossed the 6-block/SM register threshold (Block Limit Registers: 6->5)
   - Occupancy collapsed: 80.43% -> 71.01% achieved

2. LDS.128 vectorization broken on A compute loads
   - Accepted SASS: LDS.128 for all 4 contiguous A values
   - Swizzle SASS: Mixed LDS.64 / LDS.32 pattern
   - The non-linear row access defeated the compiler's vectorization
   - shared_ld instruction count likely increased (not fully measured)

The swizzle addressed store bank conflicts but at the cost of:
- Breaking the LDS.128 compute load path
- Increasing register pressure past the occupancy threshold
- Net ~57% slowdown despite unchanged shared memory footprint

Bank conflict data: Not collected in this NCU run (occupancy + LDS regressions are decisive)

========================================
Correctness
========================================
All 9 shapes PASS (256, 5×1024/1000 variants, 768, 512, K1002, N1020)
max_rel=0.000657 max_abs=0.000001 outliers=0/65536 at 256
Signature matches accepted exactly.

========================================
Conclusion
========================================
REJECTED: The specific AsT-only XOR swizzle with
physical_row = logical_row ^ (((k >> 2) & 3) << 3)
causes:
- Register pressure 40->42, crossing 6->5 blocks/SM threshold
- LDS.128 vectorization broken on A compute loads
- Occupancy 80.4%->71.0%
- Performance 57% worse

This mapping is not viable. The swizzle arithmetic cost exceeds
the bank conflict benefit for this kernel's register budget.

